559279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559652 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559702 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559713 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559752 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559782 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-(-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,-1) & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{1}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0).\n$$\n\nwhich is also undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559814 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559816 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559825 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559831 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
